# Command Line, Linux/Bash, Git, SSH, and Azure CLI

## What is the Command Line?
   The command line is a text-based way of interacting with a computer system.
   Instead of using a Graphical User Interface (GUI), users enter commands to perform tasks such as navigating directories, creating files, managing applications, and interacting with servers.

## PowerShell
   PowerShell is a task automation and configuration management framework developed by Microsoft. It includes a command-line shell and a scripting language.
   PowerShell can be used to automate tasks and manage systems, including Microsoft Azure resources.

   The Az PowerShell module allows users to manage Azure resources directly from PowerShell.

## Bash
   Bash, which stands for **Bourne Again Shell**, is a command-line shell and scripting language commonly used in Linux and Unix-based systems.
   Bash allows users to interact with the operating system, execute commands, automate tasks, and run shell scripts.


# Linux and Bash Commands

## Navigation and Directory Commands
 - `ls` — Lists files and directories.
 - `ls -la` — Lists all files and directories, including hidden files, with detailed information and permissions.
 - `pwd` — Displays the current working directory.
 - `cd directory_name` — Changes to a specified directory.
 - `cd ..` — Moves to the parent directory.
 - `mkdir directory_name` — Creates a new directory.
 - `rm -rf directory_name` — Removes a directory and its contents recursively.

## File Commands
 - `touch file_name` — Creates an empty file.
 - `nano file_name` — Opens or creates a file using the Nano text editor.
 - `echo` — Prints text or the value of a variable.
 - `cp -r source destination` — Copies directories and their contents recursively.

## Script and Permission Commands
 - `chmod +x file_name` — Makes a file executable.
 - `./script.sh` — Runs a shell script located in the current directory.
 - `./` — Refers to the current directory and is commonly used before a file name when executing a file.

## System and Server Commands
 - `uname -a` — Displays detailed system information.
 - `ssh user@ip_address` — Connects securely to a remote server using SSH.

# Git Commands
Git is a distributed version-control system used to track changes in files and manage project history.
 - `git status` — Shows the status of the working directory and staging area.
 - `git add .` — Adds all changes in the current directory to the staging area.
 - `git commit -m "message"` — Creates a commit with a descriptive message.
 - `git push` — Uploads committed changes from the local repository to the remote repository.
 - `git pull` — Downloads and integrates changes from the remote repository into the local repository.
 - `git pull --rebase origin main` — Downloads changes from the `main` branch and reapplies local commits on top of them.

# SSH Commands
SSH, or Secure Shell, is used to securely connect to remote systems and authenticate with services such as GitHub.
 - `ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -C "email@example.com"` — Generates an ED25519 SSH key pair.
 - `ssh -T git@github.com` — Tests SSH authentication with GitHub.
 - `eval "$(ssh-agent -s)"` — Starts the SSH authentication agent in Git Bash.
 - `ssh-add ~/.ssh/key_name` — Adds an SSH private key to the SSH agent.
 - `ssh-add -l` — Lists the SSH keys currently loaded into the SSH agent.

# Azure CLI
Azure CLI is a command-line tool used to communicate with and manage the Microsoft Azure platform.

## Basic Azure CLI Commands
 - `az login` — Logs in to an Azure account.
 - `az --version` — Displays the installed Azure CLI version.
 - `az group create --name MyRG --location eastus` — Creates a resource group.
 - `az group list -o table` — Lists Azure resource groups in a table format.
 - `az group delete --name MyRG` — Deletes a resource group.

# Creating and Managing a Virtual Machine

## Create a Virtual Machine
A basic Azure CLI command for creating a virtual machine is:

`az vm create --resource-group MyRG --name MyVM --image Ubuntu2404`

This command creates a virtual machine and specifies the resource group, VM name, operating system image.

## List Virtual Machines

`az vm list -o table`

This displays the available virtual machines in a table format.

## Start a Virtual Machine

`az vm start --resource-group MyRG --name MyVM`

This starts a specified virtual machine.

## Stop a Virtual Machine

`az vm stop --resource-group MyRG --name MyVM`

This stops a specified virtual machine.

## Key Takeaway

Command-line tools provide a powerful way to manage systems and automate tasks. 
Bash and PowerShell provide command-line environments for interacting with systems.
Git helps track and manage changes to files, SSH provides secure remote access and authentication.
and Azure CLI allows users to manage Azure resources directly from the command line.

## Hands On
 - Start vm machine through command line.
 - Stop vm machine through command line.
 - Display information about a resources.
 - Display Resources and Resource Group.
